Texture Baking

168
00:14:28,539 --> 00:14:36,539
Texture Baking is the feature that generates maps to represent the details of a high-poly model on an optimized model.

169
00:14:36,539 --> 00:14:37,539
The use of low-poly models can also be used to generate maps to represent the details of a high-poly model on an optimized model.

170
00:14:37,539 --> 00:14:39,539
The use of low-poly models and Texture Baking is essential

171
00:14:39,539 --> 00:14:44,539
because the low-poly models have smaller data sizes than the high-poly models,

172
00:14:44,539 --> 00:14:46,539
providing an advantage in optimization.

173
00:14:46,539 --> 00:14:52,539
This is particularly beneficial since game engines, AR and other platforms

174
00:14:52,539 --> 00:14:56,539
often face challenges when calculating high-poly models.

175
00:14:56,539 --> 00:14:58,539
By using Everywhere's Texture Baking,

176
00:14:58,539 --> 00:15:01,539
without creating a separate high-poly model,

177
00:15:01,539 --> 00:15:06,539
only a low-poly model is needed to generate normal maps and ambient occlusion maps.

178
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:07,539
Only a low-poly model is needed to generate normal maps and ambient occlusion maps.

179
00:15:08,179 --> 00:15:13,620
a normal map is a type of texture map commonly used in real-time rendering for games and other

180
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:19,379
interactive platforms to represent high resolution surface details on a low resolution model without

181
00:15:19,379 --> 00:15:25,379
increasing polygon counts an ambient occlusion map is a grayscale texture used in rendering to

182
00:15:25,379 --> 00:15:32,740
replicate indirect shadows by tracing rays from each surface on a 3d model darker areas represent

183
00:15:32,740 --> 00:15:39,060
less illuminated regions while brighter areas indicate well-lit surfaces this texture map

184
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:44,740
enhances realism by approximating self-shadowing and imitating the effects of indirect light from

185
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:50,820
the environment in the everywhere's texture baking window check detail normal map and

186
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:57,379
ambient occlusion map the ray sample count for the ao map is the number of rays sampled per pixel

187
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:02,659
a higher count yields higher quality results but increases processing time

